WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.913
<v Human Services Supervisor>Can I ask you something about Tuesday? When I showed

00:00:02.913 --> 00:00:05.825
<v Human Services Supervisor>you the policy line next to your draft, how did

00:00:05.825 --> 00:00:06.408
<v Human Services Supervisor>that land?

00:00:06.758 --> 00:00:10.085
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>Honestly, my stomach dropped at first. Then it helped. It

00:00:10.085 --> 00:00:13.413
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>was the first time anyone showed me exactly where the

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.078
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>problem was.

00:00:14.428 --> 00:00:17.695
<v Human Services Supervisor>That surprises me. I almost sent you a message saying

00:00:17.695 --> 00:00:20.308
<v Human Services Supervisor>it was a good start and needed tightening.

00:00:20.658 --> 00:00:23.667
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>I would have changed a few words and sent it

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:26.676
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>back. That is what happened with my last supervisor. Everything

00:00:26.676 --> 00:00:28.482
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>was fine, until it was not.

00:00:28.832 --> 00:00:33.229
<v Human Services Supervisor>That is the trap. A warm message feels kind. But

00:00:33.229 --> 00:00:37.627
<v Human Services Supervisor>praise mostly turns attention to the person. In the largest

00:00:37.627 --> 00:00:42.024
<v Human Services Supervisor>review of feedback studies, feedback that included praise barely changed

00:00:42.024 --> 00:00:42.464
<v Human Services Supervisor>performance.

00:00:42.814 --> 00:00:45.925
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>What actually changed my draft was seeing "should consider" next

00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:49.037
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>to the word "must." I could not argue with that.

00:00:49.037 --> 00:00:51.526
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>It was about the memo, not about me.

00:00:51.876 --> 00:00:54.596
<v Human Services Supervisor>I also started by saying the guidance had to be

00:00:54.596 --> 00:00:57.316
<v Human Services Supervisor>exact and that I knew you could get it there.

00:00:57.316 --> 00:01:00.036
<v Human Services Supervisor>Did that matter, or did it sound like a script?

00:01:00.386 --> 00:01:03.432
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>It mattered. Without it, I would have wondered whether you

00:01:03.432 --> 00:01:06.477
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>had decided I was not good at this. With it,

00:01:06.477 --> 00:01:09.218
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>the criticism felt like a reason to work harder.

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:13.549
<v Human Services Supervisor>That matches the research. In classroom experiments, one sentence naming

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:17.530
<v Human Services Supervisor>high standards and belief in the student changed who revised

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:21.512
<v Human Services Supervisor>their work. It mattered most for students who had the

00:01:21.512 --> 00:01:23.104
<v Human Services Supervisor>least reason to trust.

00:01:23.454 --> 00:01:26.432
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>One thing I would change. In the meeting I just

00:01:26.432 --> 00:01:29.410
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>said okay. I needed time. The written summary and the

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.494
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>Thursday check were what made it work.

00:01:31.844 --> 00:01:35.284
<v Human Services Supervisor>That is useful. I took your okay as agreement. Next

00:01:35.284 --> 00:01:38.724
<v Human Services Supervisor>time I will offer the written version and a check

00:01:38.724 --> 00:01:40.100
<v Human Services Supervisor>before I assume anything.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.330
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>And thanks for asking me this. It makes it easier

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:46.210
<v Disability Services Program Consultant>to bring you a problem before it reaches the counties.
